tests:
  unit:
    count: 21
    covers: "The build's own decisions, each asserted where it is made. api.test.ts (4): a refused listing throws ApiError carrying 400 while a dead socket throws no ApiError at all - the whole basis of the shell's two sentences, asserted as the presence and absence of a type rather than a flag; every non-ok answer keeps its own status over 400/404/500/503; apiAddr follows the configured base URL without its scheme, so the words on screen track DEC-12's configuration rather than a literal; and the stream reports its transport going down and coming back without those being read as flow events. useFlows.test.ts (11): unreachable vs rejected from one code path, with OPPOSITE outcomes for the rows - they survive silence (discarding evidence on a transient failure is the worse error) and go with a rejection (the old list is not an answer to the query that was refused); the rejected failure names the value typed; a listing that arrives clears whichever failure preceded it; retry resolves to false when it fails, which is what lets a surface that removes itself know not to; retrying is true for the whole fetch (fork D4's half the hook owns); a dropped stream states unreachable with no keystroke and a recovered one re-asks; useFlowDetail reports a fetch that never answered, and does NOT report a 404 - that is the ring dropping the selected flow, a cause W4's absence table reserves for the pane's own design; a bumped nonce re-runs the detail fetch so the shell's retry covers the pane; and the round-1 regression - a straggling refresh cannot overwrite the answer a later one already gave. FlowList.test.tsx (6): the four absences as a table plus the precedence rule proven in both directions - a failure takes the region from BOTH empties (asserted for filtered and unfiltered), a rejection names its value and offers a grammar but no retry, the two inherited empties are unchanged with no failure, and rows on screen leave the region absent whatever the failure, which is what keeps the statement from being said twice. Notice.test.tsx and Button.test.tsx are deliberately UNEDITED: every reuse is per contract, and a component test changing would mean the design chose wrong."
  integration:
    count: 10
    covers: "ui/src/App.test.tsx, driven through a scripted TrawlerApi rather than a mocked fetch, because the bounded contract is the shell as a whole reacting to what the API does. The status field's three changes (placeholder 'status - 404 or 4xx', no inputMode, w-field not w-field-sm); a class reaching the wire as typed; a core that stops answering mid-session raising the band above rows that do not move, with the count still counting what is on screen and no empty region; nothing captured putting the SAME sentence in the row region with no band, asserted as exactly one occurrence on the page; a refused query producing the row-region rejection with the band absent, the field keeping its text and the count reading 0; retry disabling its button AND naming what is awaited in one act, then removing the band and placing focus on the list region; a retry that fails again keeping the band, re-enabling the way out and leaving focus alone; the negative that guards this epic's scope - the onboarding phase renders no band; and the two round-1 regressions, both red before their fix - a failed detail fetch leaves a refused query its own sentence, and a listing that arrives clears a latched pane failure so the band cannot stick on a live core. Suite: 820 passed, 0 failed across 46 files (789 before this WU, 31 added); pnpm lint and tsc --noEmit clean."
  e2e:
    count: 2
    covers: "ui/e2e/specs/flows.spec.ts against the real WebKit window and an ephemeral core - the two assertions the epic plan assigns to W6. (1) The status class: with a 200, a 404 and a 500 captured, ?status=4xx narrows the list to the 4xx statuses and shows no 5xx (so 'not 2xx' cannot pass for 'is 4xx'), an exact 404 still narrows to 404, and 4xz renders the stated rejection rather than an empty list or a connection failure. (2) The unreachable statement, as a PAIR, because the epic asks for the distinguishing assertion and not just the positive one: first the control - a reachable core with a filter matching nothing renders 'no flows match this filter' and explicitly not the connection statement - then the core is killed and the band appears above rows that are still there, count unchanged, with a working retry. No user act follows the kill: the dropped SSE stream is what the shell notices, which is what W4's Interaction table requires. Harness changes were needed for both: 4xx/5xx origin routes (it produced only 2xx), and an abrupt /kill, because /stop is a graceful shutdown that waits on in-flight requests and an SSE stream never finishes - it would have held open the very connection the assertion is about. e2e: 32 passing, 0 failing across both specs, re-run green after the round-1 fix. onboarding.spec.ts is UNEDITED and green, which is this epic's standing check that the re-design really left."
suite: "pnpm test 820/820 · pnpm lint clean · tsc --noEmit clean · cargo xtask ui e2e 32/32 · cargo clippy pedantic + cargo fmt clean"
rounds: "Review round 1 found three verified defects, all one mistake: the pane's failed fetch and the list's were folded into one boolean, so a latched pane failure could speak for the list, outlive the proof against it, and a straggling refresh could un-do a retry. Each got a named regression test RED FIRST before its fix (2 in App.test.tsx, 1 in useFlows.test.ts), per docs/testing-strategy.html."
